import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  title="What ImageIO gives you free — LightPhotos Blog"
  desc="One call to Apple's image library did far more for LightPhotos than I knew. When I moved to other platforms, I saw exactly how much."
  active="blogs"
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <p class="lp-article-meta"><a href="/blogs.html">Blogs</a> &middot; <time datetime="2026-07-20">July 20, 2026</time></p>
        <h1>What ImageIO gives you free, and what it costs to leave</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          One call to Apple's ImageIO did far more for LightPhotos than I knew.
          When I moved to Linux, Windows and the browser, I saw exactly how
          much.
        </p>
        <p class="lp-article-byline">Andrew Yao &middot; 7 min read</p>
      </header>

      <aside class="lp-tldr">
        <p class="lp-tldr-label">TL;DR</p>
        <p>
          On the Mac, one call to Apple's ImageIO library made RAW photos load
          fast, and I never noticed. When I brought LightPhotos to Linux,
          Windows and the browser, I had to rebuild that work by hand. My
          version was slower, missed a format and needed a patched library.
          The hardest part was making photos look the same as on the Mac
          without the camera profiles that bigger projects have. Also, Apple's
          decoder cannot open one kind of file at all. A platform library does
          more than you think, and you only find out when you leave it.
        </p>
      </aside>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 332" role="img" aria-label="Diagram: one ImageIO call on the Mac asks for an image at approximately a given size. Under it are six jobs that the call does for free. Each job shows what I had to do without ImageIO: read the tags by hand to find the RAW preview, accept 680 ms per photo for the sensor decode, fit a brightness curve to match the look, add a fixed amount of noise reduction, read the rotation myself, and HEIC, for which no pure-Rust decoder exists.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="16">ON THE MAC</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="140" y="32" width="360" height="56" rx="8"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="320" y="55" text-anchor="middle">One ImageIO call</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="320" y="75" text-anchor="middle">Give me this image at approximately this size</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="320" y1="88" x2="320" y2="104"></line>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M314 104 L326 104 L320 112 Z"></path>

          <rect class="lp-fig-panel" x="1" y="116" width="638" height="204" rx="10"></rect>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="16" y="132" width="296" height="52" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="30" y="154">Find the RAW preview</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="30" y="173">Without it: read the tags by hand</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="328" y="132" width="296" height="52" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="342" y="154">Shrink the image as it decodes</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="342" y="173">Without it: 680 ms per photo</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="16" y="192" width="296" height="52" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="30" y="214">Match Apple's look</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="30" y="233">Without it: a fitted brightness curve</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="328" y="192" width="296" height="52" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="342" y="214">Reduce noise</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="342" y="233">Without it: a fixed amount</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="16" y="252" width="296" height="52" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="30" y="274">Turn the photo upright</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="30" y="293">Without it: I read the rotation</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="328" y="252" width="296" height="52" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="342" y="274">Read HEIC files</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s lp-fig-bad" x="342" y="293">Without it: no pure-Rust decoder</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          On the Mac, ImageIO does all six jobs inside one call. On Linux,
          Windows and the browser, I rebuilt five of them myself. I could not
          rebuild HEIC support.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>What I learned</h2>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Find out what a library does for you before you have to
          leave it.</strong> For me, that meant writing the replacement and
          timing both. The docs would not have told me.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A platform library has a price, so write it down.</strong>
          The price covers speed, file formats, running on other systems, and
          bugs you cannot fix yourself. I guessed three of those four wrong.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Plan for the reference to be wrong.</strong> A test image
          that I could work out by hand was the better check.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Check the order of your processing steps.</strong> I shrank
          the image before noise reduction instead of after. That two-line
          change saved 1.1 seconds per photo.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        LightPhotos started out as a Mac-only app. When I moved it to other
        platforms, I expected the hard parts to be the window, the graphics
        and the file dialogs. They were not. The hard part was something I had
        stopped thinking about years ago: reading an image file.
      </p>

      <h2>The one call</h2>
      <p>
        ImageIO is Apple's built-in image library. It has a function that
        means "give me this image, already shrunk to approximately this size".
        You do not load the full image and resize it yourself. The size is
        part of the request.
      </p>
      <p>
        For a JPEG, that is useful. For a camera RAW file, it makes a very
        large difference. A RAW file holds the raw data from the camera
        sensor, not a finished picture. Turning that data into full colour
        takes a lot of work.
      </p>
      <p>
        Almost every RAW file also carries a small JPEG preview. The camera
        writes it so that its own screen has something to show. If you ask
        ImageIO for a small version, it finds that preview and skips the
        sensor processing entirely. On Sony ARW files, I measured 11 ms
        instead of 170 to 210.
      </p>
      <p>I did not write any of that. I got it from one function argument.</p>

      <h2>No other platform has that function</h2>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 234" role="img" aria-label="Comparison: on macOS, one ImageIO call does scaled decoding. On other platforms, the program reads the EXIF tags to find an embedded preview. If that fails, it does a full sensor decode.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">MACOS</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-panel" x="0" y="22" width="290" height="192" rx="10"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="26" y="90" width="238" height="56" rx="8"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="145" y="113" text-anchor="middle">One call</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="145" y="133" text-anchor="middle">scaling is part of the request</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="145" y="180" text-anchor="middle">11 ms to a RAW preview</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="350" y="12">LINUX, WINDOWS, BROWSER</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-panel" x="350" y="22" width="290" height="192" rx="10"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="430" y="40" width="130" height="28" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="495" y="59" text-anchor="middle">RAW file</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="495" y1="68" x2="495" y2="76"></line>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M489 76 L501 76 L495 84 Z"></path>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="375" y="88" width="240" height="28" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="495" y="107" text-anchor="middle">Read the TIFF and EXIF tags</text>
          <path class="lp-fig-line" d="M495 116 V128 H424 V136 M495 128 H566 V136"></path>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M418 136 L430 136 L424 144 Z M560 136 L572 136 L566 144 Z"></path>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="360" y="144" width="128" height="46" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="424" y="164" text-anchor="middle">Decode the</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="424" y="179" text-anchor="middle">embedded JPEG</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="502" y="144" width="128" height="46" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="566" y="164" text-anchor="middle">rawler:</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="566" y="179" text-anchor="middle">demosaic the sensor</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="424" y="206" text-anchor="middle">fast path</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="566" y="206" text-anchor="middle">fallback</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          I could not find a ready-made equivalent on any other platform. I
          checked before I committed to the move. Everywhere I looked, the
          answer was to build it yourself.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        To build it yourself, you have to learn a few things. Most RAW files
        are really TIFF files underneath. A TIFF file is a list of labelled
        entries (tags), and the preview sits at a known spot in that list. I
        know all of that now. Honestly, I would rather not have needed to.
      </p>

      <div class="lp-compare-wrap">
        <table class="lp-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col"></th>
              <th scope="col">macOS, ImageIO</th>
              <th scope="col">Linux, Windows, browser</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Embedded RAW preview</th>
              <td>11 ms, built in</td>
              <td>Hand-rolled from EXIF tags</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Sensor decode to screen size</th>
              <td>170 to 210 ms</td>
              <td>680 ms, after optimising</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Matching Apple's look</th>
              <td>Built in</td>
              <td>A brightness curve I fitted on Sony files</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">HEIC</th>
              <td>Yes</td>
              <td>No pure-Rust decoder exists</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Linear DNG</th>
              <td>Cannot decode it</td>
              <td>Byte-exact against ground truth</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>Two steps in the wrong order</h2>
      <p>
        That 680 ms started out as 1.80 seconds. My code did noise reduction
        on the full-size image from the sensor, and only shrank it afterwards.
      </p>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 176" role="img" aria-label="Pipeline comparison: noise reduction at 24 megapixels before the shrink takes 1.80 seconds. The shrink before noise reduction takes 0.68 seconds.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">A 2560 PX PREVIEW FROM A 24 MP SONY ARW</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="0" y="26" width="142" height="32" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="71" y="46" text-anchor="middle">Decode sensor</text>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M146 36 L146 48 L156 42 Z"></path>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box lp-fig-bad-stroke" x="160" y="26" width="142" height="32" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="231" y="46" text-anchor="middle">Denoise at 24 MP</text>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M306 36 L306 48 L316 42 Z"></path>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="320" y="26" width="142" height="32" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="391" y="46" text-anchor="middle">Shrink to 2560</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar-dim" x="0" y="66" width="462" height="7" rx="3.5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="476" y="52">1.80 s &middot; peak 1.39 GB</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="0" y="106" width="142" height="32" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="71" y="126" text-anchor="middle">Decode sensor</text>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M146 116 L146 128 L156 122 Z"></path>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="160" y="106" width="142" height="32" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="231" y="126" text-anchor="middle">Shrink to 2560</text>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M306 116 L306 128 L316 122 Z"></path>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="320" y="106" width="142" height="32" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="391" y="126" text-anchor="middle">Denoise at 2560</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar" x="0" y="146" width="175" height="7" rx="3.5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="476" y="132">0.68 s &middot; peak 1.21 GB</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          I swapped two lines. At full size, the output is identical in every
          byte.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Swapping those two steps was a two-line change, and it saved 1.1
        seconds per photo.
      </p>

      <h2>The other costs</h2>
      <p>
        Speed was the first cost, and file formats were the second. ImageIO
        reads HEIC, the format that iPhones use. Nobody has written a HEIC
        reader in pure Rust, the language LightPhotos uses. My only option was
        to hook into a C library, and that meant extra build tools on every
        platform. I was trying to keep those platforms simple to build. So
        HEIC still only works on the Mac, and you can see that difference as a
        user.
      </p>
      <p>
        The third cost was a library that would not run in a browser. The RAW
        library reads the system clock to log how long each photo took to
        load. In the browser (under WebAssembly), no clock exists behind that
        call, so it crashes. My build now downloads the library, applies a
        four-line patch and builds from that copy. Cargo, the Rust build tool,
        cannot apply a patch for only the browser build, so every build uses
        the patched copy.
      </p>

      <h2>Making the other platforms look like the Mac</h2>
      <p>
        Opening a RAW file on Linux, Windows and in the browser was only half
        the job. The photo also had to look the same as it does on the Mac.
        That was the hardest part of the whole move.
      </p>
      <p>
        Bigger photo projects have camera profiles. Each profile is tuned for
        one camera model and describes how that camera records colour. I do
        not have those. All I have is the basic colour data that rawler, the
        open source RAW library I use, keeps for each camera. My first version
        on the other platforms looked flat. It was also approximately 1.45
        times darker than the same photo on the Mac.
      </p>
      <p>These are the steps I did by hand to close that gap.</p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Use what the camera recorded.</strong> I apply the white
          balance that the camera saved when you took the shot. Then I convert
          the camera colours into normal screen colours with the numbers that
          rawler has for that camera.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Handle the brightest parts the way Apple does.</strong> I
          pull back colours that are too bright for the screen gently instead
          of cutting them off. The very brightest tones go to pure white, and
          ImageIO does the same.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Trim the edges.</strong> A sensor has a border of pixels
          that are not part of the picture. I was not trimming all of them.
          Those dark edges made the browser version visibly darker than the
          Mac.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Turn the photo the right way up.</strong> The RAW library
          always reported photos as upright, so I read the rotation from the
          photo metadata myself. Before I did that, some previews came out
          sideways.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Reduce noise.</strong> ImageIO cleans up noise automatically
          when it opens a RAW file. I added a fixed amount of noise reduction
          to replace it.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Brightness was the biggest piece. At first I tuned it by eye with a
        brightness and contrast adjustment. The result got close but never
        looked quite right. So I wrote a small tool that opens the same RAW
        file twice, once with ImageIO and once with my own code. The tool then
        compares how bright the two results are. I used it to fit a brightness
        curve.
      </p>
      <p>
        Across 148 Sony files, the tool aligned my output with Apple's at 17
        points from black to white. I kept the middle value at each point. On
        the ten files I checked, my version had been 1.34 to 1.51 times darker
        than the Mac. With the curve, it is within 2% either way.
      </p>
      <p>
        It is still not a perfect match, and I want to be honest about that.
        I only fitted the curve on Sony files, because I shoot with Sony.
        Photos from other cameras may not match as closely. The curve fixes
        brightness and contrast, not colour, so a shade can still come out a
        little different. Fujifilm cameras with X-Trans sensors also get a
        softer result than they would on the Mac.
      </p>

      <h2>Apple's decoder is not always right</h2>
      <p>
        Part of my plan was an accuracy check. I would open a test file both
        ways, confirm that the results match, and treat Apple's result as the
        correct one.
      </p>
      <p>
        Apple's decoder could not open the file. It cannot read Linear DNG
        files at all.
      </p>
      <p>
        So I rebuilt the test around an answer I worked out myself. I made an
        artificial DNG file that holds a known mathematical gradient. Then I
        calculated every pixel by hand. The Rust decoder matched exactly, with
        zero mismatches across 147,456 samples.
      </p>
      <p>
        If two decoders agree, that only tells me that they agree. When one
        matches numbers I worked out myself, I know that it is right.
      </p>

      <h2>I would use it again</h2>
      <p>
        I still use ImageIO on the Mac, and it gives you a lot. It gives you
        decades of support for many file formats. It gives you a fast shortcut
        that I would never have thought to build. It also gives correct
        results on formats that are mostly reverse-engineered. Each camera
        maker adds its own quirks to those formats.
      </p>
      <p>
        I rebuilt a small piece of ImageIO myself, and now I respect it much
        more. Apple has done this work for years, across a huge list of
        cameras. On the Mac, I get all of it from one call. Thank you, Apple.
      </p>
      <p>
        The price is that ImageIO only works on Apple's platforms, and you
        cannot fix some of its bugs. Apple's decoder cannot open a Linear DNG,
        and I cannot patch it. The only workaround is to ship a second
        decoder. I do not control that code. I just live with whatever it
        does.
      </p>

